# Rune v1 — Final Architectural Corrections & Locked Decisions

This document supersedes earlier ambiguities and locks the final implementation direction for Rune v1.

---

# 1. Desktop Runtime Decision

## FINAL DECISION

Rune will use:

* Tauri v2
* Svelte
* TailwindCSS
* Go sidecar backend

Wails v2 is no longer part of the architecture.

---

## Rationale

Rune is a UX-centric desktop utility requiring:

* transparent windows
* borderless overlays
* compositor-aware rendering
* global hotkeys
* low idle memory
* premium Linux desktop integration

Tauri v2 provides stronger support for:

* modern Linux window behavior
* transparency handling
* shell integration
* lightweight runtime characteristics

The Go backend remains isolated as a sidecar process responsible for:

* crawling
* indexing
* query execution
* filesystem monitoring
* LMDB interaction

IPC occurs through:

* Unix domain sockets (preferred)
* lightweight internal IPC bridge

HTTP localhost APIs are acceptable during early development only.

---

# 2. Filesystem Monitoring Architecture

## FINAL DECISION

Rune v1 uses:

* inotify as the PRIMARY monitoring strategy
* reconciliation scans as mandatory recovery logic
* fanotify as an OPTIONAL future enhancement

fanotify is NOT required for v1.

---

## Rationale

fanotify solves large-scale watch exhaustion elegantly but introduces:

* CAP_SYS_ADMIN complexity
* packaging friction
* distro-specific privilege handling
* user trust issues
* deployment instability

The v1 priority is:

* practical deployability
* normal-user installation
* predictable behavior across Linux desktops

Rune must install and run without privileged daemons.

---

## inotify Strategy

The monitoring layer MUST:

* recursively register watches
* gracefully handle descriptor exhaustion
* detect queue overflow
* recover through scoped rescans
* avoid full filesystem reindexing

If watcher limits are exceeded:

* prioritize frequently accessed directories
* degrade gracefully
* surface internal warnings
* preserve search correctness through reconciliation

---

## Recovery Model

When overflow occurs:

DO:

* schedule subtree reconciliation
* revalidate affected paths incrementally
* preserve existing indexes

DO NOT:

* destroy indexes
* full-reindex the filesystem
* block query responsiveness

---

# 3. Query Execution Rules

## FINAL QUERY PIPELINE

Every query follows:

keydown
→ normalize
→ prefix trie lookup
→ optional trigram intersection
→ ranking
→ top-N heap
→ render

---

## Query Cancellation

All queries MUST be cancellable.

Only the newest query may render results.

Older queries still executing MUST:

* terminate early
* discard partial work
* avoid stale UI updates

This is mandatory for responsive search-as-you-type UX.

---

## Query Debounce

The previous 30ms debounce is removed.

Rune must instead use:

* 5–10ms soft debounce
  OR
* frame-coalesced dispatch

The system must feel visually instantaneous to fast typists.

30ms dispatch delay is considered perceptible.

---

## Result Cap

Result limits are reduced.

The query engine should return:

* maximum 50–100 ranked results

NOT 200.

The UI visually displays far fewer results than 200.

Smaller caps improve:

* latency
* cache locality
* ranking quality
* predictable performance

---

## Short Query Policy

Queries shorter than 3 characters MUST:

* use prefix search only
* skip trigram evaluation entirely

Reason:

Single-character trigram searches explode posting-list size and destabilize latency.

Examples:

* e
* a
* s

must never trigger full trigram search.

---

# 4. Prefix Trie Constraints

## FINAL MEMORY RULE

Trie nodes store:

* compact file IDs only

Trie nodes MUST NOT store:

* full metadata
* file structs
* path strings
* duplicated filenames

Metadata lookup occurs separately through:

* compact metadata arrays
* intern pools
* LMDB-backed persistence

This is mandatory for maintaining:

* sub-500MB RSS targets
* cache locality
* predictable traversal performance

---

# 5. TODO / Placeholder Policy Revision

The original rule:

"No TODO comments"

is replaced with:

## FINAL RULE

No TODO/FIXME/HACK comments are permitted on:

* production-critical paths
* hot query paths
* mutation pipelines
* watcher logic
* persistence layers

Non-critical tracked TODOs are acceptable when:

* explicitly scoped
* issue-linked
* not affecting correctness

The intent is:

* prohibit fake implementations
* prohibit hidden unfinished critical behavior

NOT:

* prohibit all future improvement markers

---

# 6. Frosted Glass / Transparency Policy

## FINAL UI POLICY

Blur and translucency are progressive enhancements.

Rune MUST still feel premium without compositor blur support.

---

## Preferred Rendering

If compositor supports blur:

* transparent Tauri window
* CSS backdrop-filter
* soft shadows
* subtle borders

---

## Fallback Rendering

If blur unsupported:

* semi-transparent dark surface
* preserved spacing hierarchy
* preserved typography hierarchy
* opaque fallback rendering

The UX must remain:

* fast
* minimal
* visually coherent

without blur.

---

# 7. Cold Start UX Policy

Rune MUST provide partial usability during initial indexing.

Users must NEVER experience:

* blank UI
* frozen state
* "wait until indexing finishes"

During startup:

* partial results stream incrementally
* indexing progress is visible
* search remains interactive

Target:

* usable results within 5 seconds
* even on cold index initialization

---

# 8. Memory Architecture Clarification

The memory budget:

* <500MB RSS target at 1M indexed files

is only achievable if ALL of the following are enforced:

* path interning
* compact uint32 posting lists
* immutable read-heavy structures
* lazy metadata hydration
* no duplicated strings
* no per-query allocations

Naive object-heavy Go structures WILL exceed memory limits.

Compact layout enforcement belongs in ARCHITECTURE.md.

---

# 9. Final Locked Architectural Identity

Rune is:

* a Linux-native systems utility
* RAM-first query engine
* LMDB-backed persistent indexer
* keyboard-first desktop launcher
* Everything-inspired instant filename search tool

Rune is NOT:

* an AI search engine
* a semantic indexer
* a cloud platform
* a distributed service
* an Electron application
* a full-text search engine

---

# 10. Final Product Experience Goal

Rune should feel:

* instantaneous
* invisible
* lightweight
* native
* premium
* keyboard-centric

The final experience target is:

“The results appear before the user finishes typing.”
